welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is professor Patrick kors an  assistant professor of history at Yale  professor course teaches courses in u.s.  international history and the history of  European and international politics he  is the author of the unfinished piece  after world war one professor kors is  currently working on a history of the  Pax Americana which re appraises  American pursuits of a new world order  from their origins to the Cold War and  explores how far they contributed to the  emergence of a more legitimate  international system welcome professor  course thank you thanks for having me  sure let's begin with your first book  the unfinished piece after world war one  what is its premise well it's premise  and short is that it in my  interpretation it would be misleading to  regard the ear after World War one as  one marked by the illusion of peace by  the failure of making any kind of peace  what I'm looking at is rather the  construction of something that emerged  in the 1920s as a transatlantic piece  system but I also argue and that is  somewhat different from previous  interpretations that one shouldn't focus  too much on the peace negotiations at  Versailles in 1919 in looking for the  origins of this piece but rather look at  efforts that were made in the 1920s to  reform what was originally and not so  stable international order at Versailles  and that is what I'm looking at and I'm  particularly interested in ria praising  the role that the United States and  Britain played in this process ok and  what drew you to this topic um in a way  I've always been generally interested in  origins of wars and what made for  workable lasting peace systems  especially in the 20th century and and  this book offers in some ways an  explanation of white came to a second  world war and that's of course a big  question and that has sort of had my in  court my interest for for a long time  one particular I think point here is  that I've long been interested in  transatlantic relations and in the role  that the United States played in the  emergence of a really global  international order from the 19th  century onwards to the era of the Cold  War and so in all of this I thought the  year after World War one was an  interesting one to focus on all these  interests that I had okay I imagine that  there is quite a bit of archival  material out there tell us a little bit  about the research you needed to do for  the book yes it's at once I guess quite  challenging but also a lot of fun in a  way because I I think that to be really  valuable nowadays history should be  written in this kind of area as  genuinely international history which  means that one has to go to a lot of  archives in different countries and my  projects are this mainly involves  archival research in the United States  but also in Europe in particular in  Britain and France and in Germany it  requires me to go to Paris on a regular  basis so there are many sacrifices  involved yes tough i'm sure in writing  the book did it did you think that  perhaps there are any lessons to be  learned here for instance regarding like  the Middle East conflicts and other  regions that are in conflict I would say  so yes I mean it is always difficult to  draw lessons from historical periods and  to find very exact analogies but I think  one of the things I'm trying to analyze  in this book is what I regard as the  emergence of a franco-german peace  process with two very distinct states  and sets of interest in German wyman  Germany of the time and France from  having a lot of conflicts over salient  issues and so I'm not least looking at  the question what an outside broker a  third force a kind of arbiter can do and  in this particular case what Britain and  the United States could do to in a way  enable such a piece  to have any kind of success and this of  course requires not least taking over  international commitments and the United  States after the first world war was a  power after the treaty negotiations of  versailles and the rejection of the  treaty of SI by the American Congress  that seemingly did not want to take on  and the international commitments to  security to European reconstruction but  as my book I think shows to a certain  extent this is partly a myth so the the  difficulties involved for power to  actually play a constructive  international brokering role and I think  a central theme of the book and I think  this is something that if we look at the  Middle East nowadays or efforts to for  example reform international financial  order that seemed to be quite on the  agenda a lot of lessons can be drawn  from the successes but also of course  notably the failures of the interwar  period okay and you're working on a new  book a history of the Pax Americana did  the idea of that book grow out of this  first book yes to a certain extent I  would like to say first of all that the  Pax Americana is in quotation marks so  it's it's it's it's a book about an idea  but also the translation of the idea of  a kind of American peace such as Woodrow  Wilson tried to promote in nineteen  eighteen nineteen nineteen but also  successive generations under the  Roosevelt administration the Truman  administration's try to promote and yes  in a way the the period that I'm  especially interested in this marked by  two world wars and two attempts to  re-order into the international system  and the United States played a very  marked role in both attempts so this  book is a really a an attempt to to try  to look at this period in one coherent  analysis which is quite somewhat of an  undertaking but I'm especially  interested in these you know lessons and  learning processes that you mentioned  what was different after 1945 how far  did for example American policymakers  try to draw lessons from the failures of  their side from the failures of  stabilizing for example a democratic  Germany in the 1920s and what did they  try to do differently under the  conditions of the emerging Cold War  so clearly there's a relation between  the first book in the second book all  bored very boring maybe not at all so in  terms of you know the new book that  you're working on I know you're in the  process of writing it can you share any  any any of your findings at this point  any highlights that we can look forward  to reading about um I think it's it's  really an ongoing process but I am I've  become ever more interested in looking  at what i would call the history of  ideas not just the ideas of let's say  you know the big met the big three and  at versailles or the great statesman but  also ideas that were brought forward by  non-governmental groups peace activists  others who try to have an influence of  how the United States redefined its  international role and to compare you  know what some of these groups the leak  to enforce peace or others try to have  in terms of influence on the  international system and compare that  with what for example State Planning  State Department planning amount or two  and I think this is a very quite a  complex history but but also a very kind  of rewarding history it's it tries to  open the view on international history  as not being just maybe Otto stuffy old  boring diplomatic history but a more  comprehensive international history  these are not very precise findings but  i think that's that's sort of what I'm  particularly working on at this point  okay and do you have any kind of a  timeline for the new book when can we  expect it I have I'll be on leave next  year so I hope to make white some  headway on this book then I would be  somewhat surprised if I actually managed  to finish it because it's quite a big  broken in the making but in the it's it  should come out in the foreseeable  future okay wonderful well thank you so  much for being with us here today and  sharing some research thank you I really  appreciate the opportunity okay for more  information about professor cores and  his work please visit our website at  yale edu backslash macmillan report  please be sure to join us again for  another episode of the macmillan report  made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Betty McMillan  Center for International and area  studies  you
 

